M="biochem-metab-4"
SUB="Metabolism"
T={
"faox":[("The rate-limiting step of fatty acid oxidation is:",["Transport by the carnitine shuttle","The first dehydrogenation step","Thiolytic cleavage of the chain","Activation of the fatty acid to acyl-CoA"],0,"The inner mitochondrial membrane is impermeable to CoA, so carnitine must carry the acyl group across."),
 ("Malonyl-CoA inhibits carnitine palmitoyltransferase I, which prevents:",["Burning fatty acids while they are being made","Ketone body formation in the liver","Cholesterol synthesis from acetyl-CoA","Glycogen breakdown during exercise"],0,"It is the switch that stops synthesis and oxidation running at the same time."),
 ("Which fatty acids can enter mitochondria without the carnitine shuttle?",["Short and medium chain","Long chain ones only","Unsaturated ones only","Odd-chain ones only"],0,"This is why primary carnitine deficiency impairs long-chain oxidation specifically.",2)],
"mcad":[("The hallmark of medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency is:",["Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia","Hyperketotic hypoglycaemia","Lactic acidosis with normal glucose","Hyperammonaemia without acidosis"],0,"Fatty acids cannot be burned, so ketones cannot be made when glucose runs out."),
 ("Symptoms in MCAD deficiency are typically triggered by:",["Prolonged fasting or an illness","A meal very high in protein","Exposure to an oxidant drug","Ingestion of dietary fructose"],0,"While fed, glucose covers energy needs and the defect stays silent."),
 ("A 14-month-old is found unresponsive after a vomiting illness with no oral intake. Glucose is 24 mg/dL and urinary ketones are negative. Which is the best immediate treatment?",["Intravenous dextrose","A ketogenic diet","High-dose thiamine","Protein restriction"],0,"Hypoketotic hypoglycaemia points to a fatty acid oxidation defect. Glucose both corrects the hypoglycaemia and removes the need to oxidise fat.",3)],
"fasyn":[("The rate-determining enzyme of fatty acid synthesis is:",["Acetyl-CoA carboxylase","Fatty acid synthase","Citrate lyase","HMG-CoA reductase"],0,"It uses biotin and is activated by insulin and citrate."),
 ("Carbon for fatty acid synthesis leaves the mitochondrion as:",["Citrate","Malate","Acetoacetate","Pyruvate"],0,"Citrate also signals that energy is abundant, activating the carboxylase and inhibiting PFK-1."),
 ("NADPH used for fatty acid synthesis comes mainly from:",["The HMP shunt","The electron transport chain","Beta-oxidation","The TCA cycle"],0,"Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase is the main supplier, which links carbohydrate surplus to fat storage.",2)],
"ketone":[("Ketone bodies are synthesised in which organ?",["Liver","Kidney","Muscle","Adipose tissue"],0,"They are made in hepatic mitochondria and exported to peripheral tissues."),
 ("Why can the liver not use the ketone bodies it makes?",["It lacks thiophorase","It lacks mitochondria","It lacks HMG-CoA lyase","It cannot take up beta-hydroxybutyrate"],0,"Without the CoA transferase the liver cannot activate acetoacetate, so it exports the product rather than consuming it."),
 ("A patient in severe diabetic ketoacidosis has only weakly positive urinary ketones. The best explanation is:",["The strip misses beta-hydroxybutyrate","The kidneys are not yet excreting the ketones","The ketones have already been metabolised","Acetone interferes with the strip reaction"],0,"A highly reduced state favours beta-hydroxybutyrate, which the nitroprusside strip misses, so ketonuria understates the severity.",3)],
"fedfast":[("After about 24 hours of fasting, blood glucose is maintained mainly by:",["Gluconeogenesis","Hepatic glycogenolysis","Muscle glycogenolysis","Ketone production"],0,"Liver glycogen is largely exhausted by then."),
 ("Why can the brain not use fatty acids directly?",["They cross the blood-brain barrier poorly","Neurons have no mitochondria at all","Neurons lack the beta-oxidation enzymes","Fatty acids are directly toxic to neurons"],0,"This is why the shift to ketone bodies matters in prolonged starvation."),
 ("The metabolic value of the brain switching to ketones during prolonged starvation is that it:",["Spares muscle protein from gluconeogenesis","Reduces the need for oxygen","Prevents ketoacidosis","Allows red cells to use ketones too"],0,"Less amino acid is withdrawn from muscle, which markedly extends survival. Red cells have no mitochondria and still need glucose.",3)],
"chol":[("The rate-limiting enzyme of cholesterol synthesis is:",["HMG-CoA reductase","HMG-CoA synthase","HMG-CoA lyase","Squalene epoxidase"],0,"It is the target of the statins."),
 ("How do statins lower plasma LDL?",["Hepatocytes upregulate LDL receptors","They bind LDL directly in plasma","They block intestinal cholesterol uptake","They inhibit lipoprotein lipase"],0,"Falling intracellular cholesterol increases receptor expression, which clears LDL from the blood."),
 ("HMG-CoA is a substrate for both cholesterol and ketone synthesis. What separates the two pathways?",["Their subcellular location","The cofactor each requires","The hormone that activates them","The tissue in which they occur"],0,"Cholesterol synthesis uses cytosolic HMG-CoA reductase; ketogenesis uses mitochondrial HMG-CoA synthase and lyase.",3)],
"lipenz":[("Lipoprotein lipase requires which activator?",["Apo C-II","Apo B-100","Apo E","Apo A-I"],0,"Deficiency of either LPL or apo C-II produces the same picture of very high triglycerides."),
 ("LCAT esterifies cholesterol on which particle?",["HDL","LDL","Chylomicron","VLDL"],0,"Esterification makes cholesterol more hydrophobic so it moves into the particle core and cannot escape."),
 ("Which pair of enzymes acts in opposite metabolic states?",["Lipoprotein and hormone-sensitive lipase","LCAT and CETP in the circulation","Pancreatic and hepatic lipases","Lipoprotein lipase and LCAT"],0,"LPL stores fat after a meal; hormone-sensitive lipase releases it during fasting under glucagon and adrenaline.",2)],
"lipoprot":[("Which lipoprotein carries the most cholesterol?",["LDL","Chylomicron","VLDL","HDL"],0,"Chylomicrons carry the most triglyceride; LDL carries the most cholesterol."),
 ("Reverse cholesterol transport is performed by:",["HDL","LDL","IDL","Chylomicron remnants"],0,"HDL collects excess cholesterol from tissues and returns it to the liver."),
 ("Nascent VLDL is secreted with apo B-100 but still needs apo C-II and apo E. Where does it obtain them?",["From circulating HDL","From the intestinal mucosa","By hepatic synthesis after secretion","From chylomicron remnants"],0,"HDL acts as the circulating reservoir of apolipoproteins, which is why it is central to the whole system.",3)],
"apo":[("Apo B-100 is the ligand for:",["The LDL receptor","Lipoprotein lipase","LCAT","The scavenger receptor"],0,"Defects in either apo B-100 or the receptor itself cause familial hypercholesterolaemia."),
 ("Apo B-48 and apo B-100 differ because:",["Intestinal RNA editing truncates the transcript","They are encoded by separate genes","One is glycosylated and one is not","They are cleaved by different proteases"],0,"The same gene yields a shorter intestinal form about 48 percent the length of the hepatic one."),
 ("Which apolipoprotein mediates hepatic uptake of chylomicron remnants?",["Apo E","Apo C-II","Apo A-I","Apo B-48"],0,"Apo E is the return ticket to the liver; the E-2 isoform binds poorly and causes type III hyperlipoproteinaemia.",2)],
"dyslip":[("Familial hypercholesterolaemia (type IIa) is caused by a defect in:",["The LDL receptor","Lipoprotein lipase","Apo C-II","Apo E"],0,"It is autosomal dominant, with tendon xanthomas and premature coronary disease."),
 ("Type I hyperchylomicronaemia causes pancreatitis but not accelerated atherosclerosis because:",["Chylomicrons are too large to enter the arterial wall","Triglycerides are not atherogenic in any form","HDL is simultaneously elevated","The condition presents too early in life"],0,"Only smaller particles such as LDL and remnants penetrate the intima."),
 ("A patient has very low plasma lipids, fat malabsorption, fat-soluble vitamin deficiency and acanthocytes on the blood film. Which defect explains this?",["Absent microsomal transfer protein","An absent or defective LDL receptor","Homozygosity for the apo E-2 isoform","Deficiency of lipoprotein lipase"],0,"Abetalipoproteinaemia: without MTP neither apo B-48 nor apo B-100 particles can be assembled, so no chylomicrons or VLDL are secreted.",3)],
}
